# D-106 (REVIEW eb60712, fable_pc's P42; implements port slice 2): a method `op` at the top level and the
# callable formal `op(fn: (int: x) int)` of `apply` share a name.  The formal is the innermost name:
# inside `apply`, `op(value)` calls what `apply` was given -- `apply(inc 10)` is 11 -- while `op(1 2)`
# outside is the method of two inputs, 103.  Before, `op(value)` inside `apply` resolved to the method
# (the unit's name was asked first), and a method of the formal's own signature was called silently.
# Success is 7.
fn: op (int: a; int: b) int
    return: a + b + 100
end: op

fn: inc (int: x) int
    return: x + 1
end: inc

fn: apply (op(fn: (int: x) int) int(value)) int
    return: op(value)
end: apply

int: ok 7
if: apply(inc 10) != 11
    ok: 1
if: op(1 2) != 103
    ok: 2
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
